steps.body:
*Functions* put declarations in a header and said `#include` pastes them in. Here's how that works across files. `#include "prices.h"` replaces its own line with the text of `prices.h`, exactly as if you'd typed it there. A `.cpp` file plus everything its `#include` lines paste in is a **translation unit**, and the compiler turns each one into an object file alone, never seeing the others. A small program in three files:

```cpp norun
// prices.h: the declarations every user of these functions needs
#pragma once

double midPrice(double bid, double ask);
double spreadBps(double bid, double ask);
```

```cpp norun
// prices.cpp: the one definition of each
#include "prices.h"

double midPrice(double bid, double ask) {
    return (bid + ask) / 2.0;
}

double spreadBps(double bid, double ask) {
    return (ask - bid) / midPrice(bid, ask) * 10000.0;
}
```

```cpp norun
// main.cpp
#include <iostream>
#include "prices.h"

int main() {
    std::cout << "mid " << midPrice(99.5, 100.5) << '\n';
    std::cout << "spread " << spreadBps(99.5, 100.5) << " bps\n";
    return 0;
}
```

The sandbox runs single files, so this one is built on the Mac. `-c` compiles one translation unit into an object file, and the last command links them:

```text
$ clang++ -std=c++17 -c prices.cpp
$ clang++ -std=c++17 -c main.cpp
$ clang++ prices.o main.o -o quotes
$ ./quotes
mid 100
spread 100 bps
```

Edit `prices.cpp`, and only it needs compiling again before the link. That's why big projects rebuild fast after a small change, and *Building Projects: Flags, Make, and CMake*, later in this section, automates it with Make.

A header can land in one translation unit twice, once directly and once through another header that includes it. Repeated declarations are harmless, but a header may also define things, like the constant `const double kTickSize = 0.01;`. Each translation unit may have its own copy of that constant, as *const, constexpr, and Where a Value Lives* explains later in this section, but a second one in the same unit is an error. Clang on the Mac says `redefinition of 'kTickSize'` and suggests the fix: `unguarded header; consider using #ifdef guards or #pragma once`. An **include guard** wraps the whole header:

```cpp norun
#ifndef PRICES_H
#define PRICES_H
// ... the header's text ...
#endif
```

The first paste finds `PRICES_H` undefined, so it defines it and keeps the text. Every later paste finds it defined and skips to `#endif`. The next lesson, *The Preprocessor: Macros and Conditional Compilation*, covers these directives. `#pragma once` does the same job in one line. It isn't in the C++ standard, but g++, clang, and MSVC all support it, and most new code uses it.

