steps.body:
*From Source to Executable: Preprocess, Compile, Link* called names like `_ZSt4cout` mangled. Without `-C`, `nm` shows the mangled names stored in `prices.o`:

```text
$ nm prices.o | grep -v ltmp
0000000000000000 T __Z8midPricedd
0000000000000028 T __Z9spreadBpsdd
```

C++ allows overloads, so `midPrice(double, double)` and `midPrice(int, int)` need different symbols. The compiler **mangles** each name: it encodes the namespace, the name, and the parameter types into one string. g++ and clang share one scheme, the Itanium C++ ABI. `_Z` starts a mangled name, `8midPrice` is the name with its length in front, and `dd` means two `double` parameters. A namespace wraps the name in `N` and `E`, so `orders::count()` is `_ZN6orders5countEv`, with `v` for no parameters. macOS adds one more `_` to the front of every symbol, so you see `__Z`. `nm -C` and the `c++filt` tool turn mangled names back into C++, and link errors show them demangled. MSVC mangles differently, as `?midPrice@@YANNN@Z`, which is one reason object files from different compilers don't link together.

C has no overloading, so a C compiler keeps the plain name. Many libraries you'll call are written in C, like a vendor's feed handler or the operating system's own interface. Declare a C function `extern "C"`, and the C++ compiler looks for its plain name. Without it, the Mac's linker can't match the two, and even guesses why:

```text
$ clang -c feed.c
$ nm feed.o | grep -v ltmp
0000000000000000 T _mid_price
$ clang++ -std=c++17 -c main.cpp
$ clang++ main.o feed.o -o feed
Undefined symbols for architecture arm64:
  "mid_price(double, double)", referenced from:
      _main in main.o
   NOTE: found '_mid_price' in feed.o, declaration possibly missing 'extern "C"'
```

With `extern "C" double mid_price(double bid, double ask);` in `main.cpp`, it links. C headers wrap their declarations in `extern "C" { }` when a C++ compiler reads them, and C++ libraries export `extern "C"` functions so that other languages can call them, as Python does in *C++ and Python Together: a C ABI and Bindings*, near the end of the course. A plain name has no room for parameter types, so an `extern "C"` function can't be overloaded:

```cpp error
#include <iostream>

extern "C" double mid_price(double bid, double ask) {
    return (bid + ask) / 2.0;
}

extern "C" int mid_price(int bid, int ask) {  // a second mid_price
    return (bid + ask) / 2;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << mid_price(99.5, 100.5) << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

example.code:
#include <cstdlib>
#include <cxxabi.h>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

// Turns a mangled symbol back into C++, as nm -C and c++filt do, using the
// demangler in g++'s own library. It needs a pointer, which the Memory section
// covers, so take it as given.
std::string demangle(std::string symbol) {
    int status = 0;
    char* text = abi::__cxa_demangle(symbol.c_str(), nullptr, nullptr, &status);
    const std::string result = (status == 0) ? text : "(not a C++ name)";
    std::free(text);
    return result;
}

// A function outside any namespace: _Z, the name's length, the name, then one
// code per parameter (i int, d double, c char, b bool, v no parameters).
std::string mangle(std::string name, std::string params) {
    return "_Z" + std::to_string(name.size()) + name + params;
}

int main() {
    const std::vector<std::string> symbols = {
        mangle("midPrice", "dd"),
        mangle("midPrice", "ii"),
        mangle("lotsFor", "i"),
        mangle("resetBook", "v"),
        "_ZN6orders5countEv",
        "mid_price",
    };
    for (const auto& s : symbols) {
        std::cout << s << " -> " << demangle(s) << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

